  {
    "subject": "physics",
    "question": "Explain a fixed pulley to a beginner in English. Use the supported 3D pulley, highlight the rope, and explain the rope, load and effort direction. Make clear that this is conceptual rather than a physical simulation. Keep the lesson to four short teaching chunks including one understanding question.",
    "elapsed_seconds": 10.45,
    "fallback_attempted": false,
    "errors": [],
    "answer": {
      "chunks": [
        {
          "speak": "A fixed pulley is a simple machine that helps us change the direction of a force. It consists of a wheel that rotates around a central axle, but the entire pulley system stays in one place, or is 'fixed'. This is a conceptual model to help you understand the principles, not a physical simulation.",
          "diagram": {
            "action": "none",
            "visual": {
              "kind": "assembly",
              "model": "pulley",
              "focus": "wheel",
              "phase": 0.5
            }
          },
          "key_point": "Fixed Pulley Basics"
        },
        {
          "speak": "Now let's look at the rope. The rope runs over the groove in the pulley's wheel. One end of this rope is attached to the 'load' \u2013 that's the object you want to lift. The load, due to gravity, always pulls downwards.",
          "diagram": {
            "action": "highlight",
            "visual": {
              "kind": "assembly",
              "model": "pulley",
              "focus": "rope",
              "phase": 0.5
            }
          },
          "key_point": "Rope and Load"
        },
        {
          "speak": "To lift the load, you apply a force called 'effort'. With a fixed pulley, if the load is pulling down on one side of the rope, you pull down on the other side of the rope. So, the effort you apply is also directed downwards, even though the load moves upwards.",
          "diagram": {
            "action": "none",
            "visual": {
              "kind": "assembly",
              "model": "pulley",
              "focus": "rope",
              "phase": 0.5
            }
          },
          "key_point": "Effort Direction"
        },
        {
          "question": "What is the main advantage of using a fixed pulley?",
          "expected_concepts": [
            "change direction of force",
            "easier to apply force downwards"
          ],
          "speak": "Based on what we've discussed, what is the main advantage of using a fixed pulley?",
          "diagram": {
            "action": "none",
            "visual": {
              "kind": "assembly",
              "model": "pulley",
              "focus": "wheel",
              "phase": 0.5
            }
          },
          "key_point": "Check Your Understanding"
        }
      ],
